          "notifications": [],
          "rules": [
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "RUSTSEC-2026-0012",
                "GHSA-3288-p39f-rqpv"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nThe `asm!` block enabled by the off-by-default `asm` feature, when enabled on ARMv8 targets, misspecified the operand\ntype for all of its operands, using `in` for pointers and values which were subsequently mutated by operations performed\nwithin the assembly block.\n\n### Impact\n\nIt's unclear what practical impact, if any, this actually had. Incorrect operand types are technically undefined\nbehavior, however changing them had no actual impact on the generated assembly for these targets. The possibility still\nexists that it may lead to potential memory safety or other issues on hypothetical future versions of rustc.\n\n### Mitigation\n\nThe operand types were changed from `in` to `inout`, and the impacted versions of the `keccak` crate were yanked.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\nThe `asm!` block enabled by the off-by-default `asm` feature, when enabled on ARMv8 targets, misspecified the operand\ntype for all of its operands, using `in` for pointers and values which were subsequently mutated by operations performed\nwithin the assembly block.\n\n### Impact\n\nIt's unclear what practical impact, if any, this actually had. Incorrect operand types are technically undefined\nbehavior, however changing them had no actual impact on the generated assembly for these targets. The possibility still\nexists that it may lead to potential memory safety or other issues on hypothetical future versions of rustc.\n\n### Mitigation\n\nThe operand types were changed from `in` to `inout`, and the impacted versions of the `keccak` crate were yanked."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [RUSTSEC-2026-0012](https://osv.dev/RUSTSEC-2026-0012)**\n(Also published as: [GHSA-3288-p39f-rqpv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-3288-p39f-rqpv), ).\n\n## [RUSTSEC-2026-0012](https://osv.dev/RUSTSEC-2026-0012)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e The `asm!` block enabled by the off-by-default `asm` feature, when enabled on ARMv8 targets, misspecified the operand\n\u003e type for all of its operands, using `in` for pointers and values which were subsequently mutated by operations performed\n\u003e within the assembly block.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e It's unclear what practical impact, if any, this actually had. Incorrect operand types are technically undefined\n\u003e behavior, however changing them had no actual impact on the generated assembly for these targets. The possibility still\n\u003e exists that it may lead to potential memory safety or other issues on hypothetical future versions of rustc.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e The operand types were changed from `in` to `inout`, and the impacted versions of the `keccak` crate were yanked.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-3288-p39f-rqpv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-3288-p39f-rqpv)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e The `asm!` block enabled by the off-by-default `asm` feature, when enabled on ARMv8 targets, misspecified the operand\n\u003e type for all of its operands, using `in` for pointers and values which were subsequently mutated by operations performed\n\u003e within the assembly block.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e It's unclear what practical impact, if any, this actually had. Incorrect operand types are technically undefined\n\u003e behavior, however changing them had no actual impact on the generated assembly for these targets. The possibility still\n\u003e exists that it may lead to potential memory safety or other issues on hypothetical future versions of rustc.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e The operand types were changed from `in` to `inout`, and the impacted versions of the `keccak` crate were yanked.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/github/workspace/prototypes/lp1-node/Cargo.lock | keccak | 0.1.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-3288-p39f-rqpv | keccak | 0.1.6 |\n| RUSTSEC-2026-0012 | keccak | 0.1.6 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`prototypes/lp1-node/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"RUSTSEC-2026-0012\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [RUSTSEC-2026-0012](https://osv.dev/RUSTSEC-2026-0012)**\n(Also published as: [GHSA-3288-p39f-rqpv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-3288-p39f-rqpv), ).\n\n## [RUSTSEC-2026-0012](https://osv.dev/RUSTSEC-2026-0012)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e The `asm!` block enabled by the off-by-default `asm` feature, when enabled on ARMv8 targets, misspecified the operand\n\u003e type for all of its operands, using `in` for pointers and values which were subsequently mutated by operations performed\n\u003e within the assembly block.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e It's unclear what practical impact, if any, this actually had. Incorrect operand types are technically undefined\n\u003e behavior, however changing them had no actual impact on the generated assembly for these targets. The possibility still\n\u003e exists that it may lead to potential memory safety or other issues on hypothetical future versions of rustc.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e The operand types were changed from `in` to `inout`, and the impacted versions of the `keccak` crate were yanked.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-3288-p39f-rqpv](https://osv.dev/GHSA-3288-p39f-rqpv)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e The `asm!` block enabled by the off-by-default `asm` feature, when enabled on ARMv8 targets, misspecified the operand\n\u003e type for all of its operands, using `in` for pointers and values which were subsequently mutated by operations performed\n\u003e within the assembly block.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e It's unclear what practical impact, if any, this actually had. Incorrect operand types are technically undefined\n\u003e behavior, however changing them had no actual impact on the generated assembly for these targets. The possibility still\n\u003e exists that it may lead to potential memory safety or other issues on hypothetical future versions of rustc.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Mitigation\n\u003e \n\u003e The operand types were changed from `in` to `inout`, and the impacted versions of the `keccak` crate were yanked.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/github/workspace/prototypes/lp1-node/Cargo.lock | keccak | 0.1.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-3288-p39f-rqpv | keccak | 0.1.6 |\n| RUSTSEC-2026-0012 | keccak | 0.1.6 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`prototypes/lp1-node/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"RUSTSEC-2026-0012\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
