              "deprecatedIds": [
                "RUSTSEC-2026-0387"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "## Summary\n\nSeveral operations intended to be constant-time could execute secret-dependent\nbranches, depending on the compiler and target CPU. These include subtraction,\nzero counting, division, and Montgomery multiplication.\n\nAdditionally, `UintRef` equality could terminate at the first unequal limb,\nand `BoxedMontyForm::invert` branched on whether an inverse exists.\n\n## Impact\n\nApplications using affected operations on secret integers may leak information\nthrough timing or microarchitectural side channels. This includes information\nabout intermediate arithmetic values, matching limbs, and invertibility.\n\nSuch leakage could potentially enable recovery of cryptographic secrets.\nPractical exploitability depends on the downstream algorithm, compiler, target\nCPU, and an attacker's ability to observe the side channel.\n\n## Resolution\n\nUpgrade to v0.7.6, which removes the identified branches, updates `ctutils` to\ninclude its constant-time fixes, and adds BINSEC-based regression checks using\n`cargo checkct` for arithmetic on multiple targets.",
                "text": "## Summary\n\nSeveral operations intended to be constant-time could execute secret-dependent\nbranches, depending on the compiler and target CPU. These include subtraction,\nzero counting, division, and Montgomery multiplication.\n\nAdditionally, `UintRef` equality could terminate at the first unequal limb,\nand `BoxedMontyForm::invert` branched on whether an inverse exists.\n\n## Impact\n\nApplications using affected operations on secret integers may leak information\nthrough timing or microarchitectural side channels. This includes information\nabout intermediate arithmetic values, matching limbs, and invertibility.\n\nSuch leakage could potentially enable recovery of cryptographic secrets.\nPractical exploitability depends on the downstream algorithm, compiler, target\nCPU, and an attacker's ability to observe the side channel.\n\n## Resolution\n\nUpgrade to v0.7.6, which removes the identified branches, updates `ctutils` to\ninclude its constant-time fixes, and adds BINSEC-based regression checks using\n`cargo checkct` for arithmetic on multiple targets."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [RUSTSEC-2026-0387](https://osv.dev/RUSTSEC-2026-0387)**.\n\n## [RUSTSEC-2026-0387](https://osv.dev/RUSTSEC-2026-0387)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Several operations intended to be constant-time could execute secret-dependent\n\u003e branches, depending on the compiler and target CPU. These include subtraction,\n\u003e zero counting, division, and Montgomery multiplication.\n\u003e \n\u003e Additionally, `UintRef` equality could terminate at the first unequal limb,\n\u003e and `BoxedMontyForm::invert` branched on whether an inverse exists.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications using affected operations on secret integers may leak information\n\u003e through timing or microarchitectural side channels. This includes information\n\u003e about intermediate arithmetic values, matching limbs, and invertibility.\n\u003e \n\u003e Such leakage could potentially enable recovery of cryptographic secrets.\n\u003e Practical exploitability depends on the downstream algorithm, compiler, target\n\u003e CPU, and an attacker's ability to observe the side channel.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Resolution\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to v0.7.6, which removes the identified branches, updates `ctutils` to\n\u003e include its constant-time fixes, and adds BINSEC-based regression checks using\n\u003e `cargo checkct` for arithmetic on multiple targets.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/github/workspace/prototypes/lp4-execution/Cargo.lock | crypto-bigint | 0.5.5 |\n| lockfile:/github/workspace/prototypes/pocold/Cargo.lock | crypto-bigint | 0.5.5 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| RUSTSEC-2026-0387 | crypto-bigint | 0.7.6 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`prototypes/lp4-execution/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"RUSTSEC-2026-0387\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`prototypes/pocold/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"RUSTSEC-2026-0387\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [RUSTSEC-2026-0387](https://osv.dev/RUSTSEC-2026-0387)**.\n\n## [RUSTSEC-2026-0387](https://osv.dev/RUSTSEC-2026-0387)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e Several operations intended to be constant-time could execute secret-dependent\n\u003e branches, depending on the compiler and target CPU. These include subtraction,\n\u003e zero counting, division, and Montgomery multiplication.\n\u003e \n\u003e Additionally, `UintRef` equality could terminate at the first unequal limb,\n\u003e and `BoxedMontyForm::invert` branched on whether an inverse exists.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications using affected operations on secret integers may leak information\n\u003e through timing or microarchitectural side channels. This includes information\n\u003e about intermediate arithmetic values, matching limbs, and invertibility.\n\u003e \n\u003e Such leakage could potentially enable recovery of cryptographic secrets.\n\u003e Practical exploitability depends on the downstream algorithm, compiler, target\n\u003e CPU, and an attacker's ability to observe the side channel.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Resolution\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to v0.7.6, which removes the identified branches, updates `ctutils` to\n\u003e include its constant-time fixes, and adds BINSEC-based regression checks using\n\u003e `cargo checkct` for arithmetic on multiple targets.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/github/workspace/prototypes/lp4-execution/Cargo.lock | crypto-bigint | 0.5.5 |\n| lockfile:/github/workspace/prototypes/pocold/Cargo.lock | crypto-bigint | 0.5.5 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| RUSTSEC-2026-0387 | crypto-bigint | 0.7.6 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`prototypes/lp4-execution/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"RUSTSEC-2026-0387\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n`prototypes/pocold/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"RUSTSEC-2026-0387\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
